  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Repertoire",
    "overview": "**Creative North Star: \"The Repertoire\"**\n\nOne performer, five staged productions of the same script. The content (name, title, tagline, experience, skills, projects, blog) never changes; a switch in the header restages it in one of five complete visual worlds: Editorial (the default), Avant-garde, Engineer, Creative, and Arcade. The system's personality is plural on purpose. Each world commits fully to its own register (the broadsheet, the dark gallery, the terminal, the studio, and the arcade cabinet). None of them is a theme laid over a neutral base, because the neutral base does not exist.\n\nUnderneath, one skeleton carries all five: a fixed translucent header, a full-viewport hero, and a single centered column of sections (About, Experience, Education, Skills, Projects, Contact) at medium density. A world declares nine semantic color tokens (page, raised, text, secondary text, accent, accent hover, border, card, card hover), two font slots, and two radii, and the shared sections re-skin themselves. Two accent-use tokens sit on top for every world: `--on-accent`, the text on an accent fill (the page color by default), and `--accent-text`, small text in the accent (the accent itself by default); a world overrides either only when its accent needs it, as Editorial does. Only Hero, Experience, and Skills get a bespoke component per world.\n\nThe aesthetic philosophy is restraint everywhere except the accent and the display type. Every world has exactly one accent and spends it on small, high-signal things: buttons, period labels, rules, hover and selection states. Display typography carries identity. Motion is one shared reveal grammar (fade and rise on scroll, once) plus a single signature per world. Every signature lives in the hero.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Five complete worlds, one content source, switched at runtime and persisted per visitor.",
      "One accent per world, spent on interactive and labeling elements only.",
      "Display typography carries identity: monospace, geometric sans, heavy grotesk, display grotesk, or slab poster.",
      "Flat, border-defined surfaces in four worlds; Creative alone lifts with shadow.",
      "Shared reveal motion (fade, 30px rise, 0.5s, once) with a signature per world, staged in the hero.",
      "WebGL in one world only (Avant-garde's particle field), mounted only while that world is active and backed by a CSS fallback.",
      "Candy stays on the cards: Arcade's candy colors are surfaces, never UI chrome.",
      "Bilingual by construction: every text node renders both locales and CSS shows one."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Accent Rule",
        "body": "Each world has exactly one accent and spends it on buttons, labels, rules, hover and selection states. Body copy, headings, and card surfaces never take the accent. Arcade's candy colors are surfaces with Cabinet Black text on them, never accents.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Own Palette Rule",
        "body": "A world uses only its own token set. No Candy Green in Engineer, no Creative shadow in Editorial, no Terminal Teal outside the terminal.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Dark Text on Bright Accent Rule",
        "body": "Text on an accent fill reads `--on-accent`; small text in the accent reads `--accent-text`. Teal, Lime, and Gold are light accents, so their fills take the page color, the default (11:1 to 16:1). Violet's own hero and filter fills take white (6.7:1). Signal Orange measures 3.0:1 on Newsprint and 3.3:1 under white text, so Editorial sets `--on-accent` to Ink (5.2:1) and `--accent-text` to Signal Orange Ink (#bf3900, 4.5:1 or more on every Editorial surface); in Editorial, `.text-accent`, `hover:text-accent`, and `group-hover:text-accent` all render in it. Raw Signal Orange stays on rules, outlines, fills, and text at 24px and up, which opts in through `.text-accent-display`.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Heading Slot Rule",
        "body": "Headings never hardcode a family. They use the heading slot (`--font-heading`) so a world can swap the voice in one token.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Loud Display, Quiet Body Rule",
        "body": "Identity lives in the display size and face. Body and label sizes stay between 0.75rem and 1.125rem in every world.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Same Bones Rule",
        "body": "Section order, container widths, and the About, Education, Projects, and Contact structures stay shared by default. A world forks a section only when its identity needs it; today that is Hero, Experience, and Skills.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The 896 Rule",
        "body": "Section content lives in an 896px column. Only the header and the hero widen to 1152px.",
        "section": "layout"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two-Radius Rule",
        "body": "A world declares exactly two radii, `--radius-card` and `--radius-btn`. Chips, tags, and filters borrow the button radius; containers borrow the card radius.",
        "section": "shapes"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do declare a new world with the nine color tokens, two font slots, and two radii on its `[data-design]` selector (overriding `--on-accent` or `--accent-text` only if its accent misses 4.5:1), register it in `src/data/designs.ts`, and add it to the anti-FOUC allowlist.",
      "Do keep the accent on buttons, labels, rules, and states (The One Accent Rule), and set text on an accent fill with `text-on-accent`, never a hardcoded white or page color.",
      "Do treat Signal Orange as a display color: rules, outlines, fills with Ink text, and text at 24px and up through `.text-accent-display`. Small orange text goes through `--accent-text` (Signal Orange Ink, #bf3900); raw Signal Orange measures 3.0:1 on Newsprint and 3.3:1 under white text.",
      "Do resolve headings through the heading slot and body through the body slot; only Engineer's labels and the shared code face name JetBrains Mono directly.",
      "Do render both locales in every text node behind `data-lang-show` so the language toggle never re-renders.",
      "Do respect `prefers-reduced-motion`, and make new motion join the reveal grammar (fade, 30px rise, 0.5s, once).",
      "Do keep bespoke sections to Hero, Experience, and Skills unless a world's identity needs another (The Same Bones Rule).",
      "Do mount a WebGL scene only while its world is active, calibrate a lighter phone build, and ship a CSS fallback, as Avant-garde does."
